WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.240
Oftentimes, it is possible to find other&nbsp;
relevant information to add to the dataset.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.880
In sales forecast, it's often not&nbsp;
sufficient to use internal data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.000
It is common to collect comparison&nbsp;
information and macroeconomic information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.080
At other times, a dataset may contain duplicate&nbsp;
information or columns that are not informative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.280
As an example, sales amount is often kept&nbsp;
in multiple currency in IT system. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.920
only sales amount in one currency should&nbsp;
be used in analysis, otherwise can cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.520
numeric difficulties in analysis. Additional&nbsp;
variables can come from several sources.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.800
First, datasets are often compiled from&nbsp;
online and public data sources. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.080
often possible to collect additional&nbsp;
information from those sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.320
Second, it is also possible and sometimes&nbsp;
necessary to create additional variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.120
from the data, including dummy variables&nbsp;
and transformed variables, and interactions.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.040
It is common to create dummy variables from&nbsp;
a single variable that takes a small number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.280
of fixed values. Each of the values is called a&nbsp;
category and the variable is called a categorical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:28.160
variable. We can create m minus 1 dummy variables,&nbsp;
from a categorical variable with m categories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.520
where m is an integer. Each dummy&nbsp;
variable takes the value of 0 or 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:40.000
where 1 usually indicates&nbsp;
yes and a 0 indicates no.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.080
Let's look at an example, the table on the left&nbsp;
shows three property types, single family home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.880
townhouse and condo. So, there are three&nbsp;
categories and m equal to 3. We can create two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.800
dummy variables D1 and D2, where D1 indicates&nbsp;
whether it is a single family home and D2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:05.840
indicates whether it is a townhouse. Both D1 and&nbsp;
D2 taking the value 0 indicates a condo, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.080
neither a single family house or a townhouse. This&nbsp;
procedure of creating dummy variables can lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.040
to a large number of dummy variables when there&nbsp;
are many categories for a categorical variable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.880
which can be problematic when observations for&nbsp;
some categories are sparse. We can systematically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.040
reduce the number of categories by combining&nbsp;
categories that are close to each other.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.120
Going back to the same example in a original&nbsp;
dataset, there are many categories other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:41.120
single family home, townhouse and the condo.&nbsp;
Depending on the purpose of our analysis we may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:47.440
choose to combine some categories. For example,&nbsp;
condo, coop, multi-family and mobile home can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.600
all be mounted to the others category. In this&nbsp;
way, we end up using only two dummy variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.440
instead of five should we not combine categories.&nbsp;
However, be caution that, how categories should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.720
be combined is not always straightforward and&nbsp;
it should be chosen carefully. It is common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.440
to combine sparse categories together, but&nbsp;
it may also depend on the problem context.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.560
As we discussed before, we may want&nbsp;
to remove variables from our data set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.600
The first motivation is to simplify model and&nbsp;
analysis. Some variables may not contain much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.480
useful information and therefore do not deserve&nbsp;
added complexity for including them in analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.600
There are also cases when some variables&nbsp;
contain the same information, and we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.960
only keep one of them. By removing variables&nbsp;
and including a smaller set of variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.520
we end up with more parsimonious models,&nbsp;
which also tend to be easier to interpret.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.320
Note that some methods do not work well&nbsp;
when the distribution for a variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.840
is degenerate, meaning that the&nbsp;
variable only takes a single value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:04:00.240
Variables with degenerate distributions&nbsp;
have to be removed for better performance.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.840
A variable with degenerative distribution is also&nbsp;
called a zero variance variable in exploratory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:12.080
data analysis. For example, if everybody in a&nbsp;
high school class are born in the same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.360
then the first year is a zero variance variable,&nbsp;
and is not a relevant variable in analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.600
including it may cause numeric difficulties. It is&nbsp;
more common to have near-zero variance variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.880
where only a very small portion of the data&nbsp;
takes a different value. If all students in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.520
the class are born in the same year except one&nbsp;
student, then the first year is a variable with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:39.120
near-zero variance. Keeping zero variable&nbsp;
or near-zero variance variables can cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.480
cardinality issues in regression analysis, where&nbsp;
coefficient estimates can change erratically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:55.840
when there are small changes to the data,&nbsp;
such behavior is clearly undesirable.

